// terrain_lem_flat_ramp.comp
// ===========================================================================
// FLAT RESOLUTION, pass 3 of 3. Combines the two geodesic distance fields into
// the ROUTING SURFACE that every downstream consumer steers by.
// ===========================================================================
//
//      route = filled + flatStep * ( 2*dLow + (highRef - dHigh) )
//
// ★★★ THE COEFFICIENTS ARE THE PROOF, not a taste setting. Along the
// relaxation parent link dLow falls by at least one cardinal step (1 cell)
// while dHigh can move by at most one diagonal step (1.4167 cells), so
//
//      delta(route) <= -2 * 1 + 1 * 1.4167  =  -0.583  <  0
//
// for every resolved flat cell. A strictly lower neighbour is therefore
// GUARANTEED TO EXIST, at any relaxation pass count. That is what stops flat
// resolution from manufacturing the terminal sinks a perturbed-surface dither
// creates (see the note in the CPU priorityFlood): the gradient is built from
// DISTANCES, never by nudging the surface with noise, so a cell can never
// accidentally end up lower than all of its neighbours.
//
// ★★★★ highRef IS A CAP, AND THE CAP IS WHAT MAKES THE PROOF HOLD ON A GPU.
//
// The `highRef - dHigh` form keeps the increment non-negative, but its real job
// is to BOUND how far the away-from-high term reaches. Without a cap the proof
// above is false on this path, and the failure is a silent one:
//
//   The high front is relaxed with the same bounded budget as the low front,
//   and the two do NOT converge together - a long plain can be 20 cells from
//   its outlet and 900 from the nearest wall. So some flat cells still hold the
//   "front never arrived" sentinel while their neighbours hold a real distance.
//   Whatever value the sentinel is mapped to, it can then sit next to a wildly
//   different real one, the dHigh term jumps by far more than 1.4167, and a
//   LOCAL SINK appears in the middle of a plain. Which is exactly the defect
//   this whole pass exists to remove, reintroduced by its own diagnostic gap.
//
// Capping at highRef closes it by construction. After k relaxation passes every
// flat cell within k cells of a high seed is resolved, so if highRef <= k - 2,
// any cell still unresolved is at least highRef from high ground - i.e. inside
// the capped region where the term is 0 anyway, and so are all its neighbours.
// A jump can therefore only happen between two RESOLVED cells, whose dHigh
// differ by at most one diagonal step. The host picks highRef accordingly.
//
// It is also the better model. Garbrecht & Martz normalise the away-from-high
// term per flat; an uncapped distance over a thousand-cell plain would tilt the
// whole plain away from one wall and swamp the outlet term. The term is a
// near-boundary effect - it decides where a tributary AIMS as it leaves the
// hillslope - and a few tens of cells is the whole of its business.
//
// ★★★ MAGNITUDE IS PHYSICAL, and that is the second half of the fix. The old
// ladder was a few ulp, which had two consequences that both read as "the
// solver ignores flats":
//
//   * Neighbour differences on a flat took only a handful of distinct float
//     values, so slope^1.5 quantized to a near-binary weight row and MFD
//     collapsed to D8 -- spreading died exactly where low gradients need it.
//   * terrain_lem_incise clamps erosion to incisionSafety * (drop to the
//     receiver). A few ulp of drop is zero drop, so a flat could not incise AT
//     ALL: no channel could form, so no channel could capture area, so the
//     A^m feedback that makes rivers merge and deepen never started. The flat
//     stayed frozen with whatever the conditioning had drawn on it, forever.
//     The mirror of this was already known and only half fixed -- see the
//     maxDepositionMeters note in TerrainManager.h, which calls a flat cell a
//     ratchet that can only GAIN height. This is the other jaw of it.
//
// flatStep comes from a real floodplain gradient in m/m, so the routing drop
// across a flat is millions of ulp: incision engages at a small but finite
// rate, the channel cuts a bed, the bed own gradient takes over, and the
// synthetic ramp stops mattering. That handover is the point of the whole
// pass -- the ramp is a seed for the feedback loop, not the answer.
#version 450
layout(local_size_x = 256) in;

layout(push_constant) uniform PC {
    int width; int height;
    float flatStep;     // normalized height per unit of increment
    float highRef;      // cells; the dHigh influence RADIUS - see below
    int countUnresolved;
} pc;

layout(set = 0, binding = 0) readonly  buffer Filled  { float filledField[]; };
layout(set = 0, binding = 1) readonly  buffer Dist    { uint  distField[]; };
layout(set = 0, binding = 2) writeonly buffer Route   { float routeField[]; };
layout(set = 0, binding = 3)           buffer Diag    { uint  diag[]; };

const float kInvScale = 1.0 / 12.0;   // fixed point -> cells
const uint  kCeil = 0xFFFDu;

void main() {
    uint ui = gl_GlobalInvocationID.x;
    if (ui >= uint(pc.width * pc.height)) return;
    int i = int(ui);

    uint c = distField[i];
    uint lo = c & 0xFFFFu;
    uint hi = c >> 16;

    float inc = 0.0;
    if (hi != 0xFFFFu) {                 // this is a flat cell
        if (lo < kCeil) {
            float dLow = float(lo) * kInvScale;
            // Unreached (sentinel) maps to the cap, which is the same value
            // every cell beyond the influence radius already has. That is what
            // puts an unresolved cell and all of its neighbours in one flat
            // region of this term instead of on a cliff of it.
            float dHigh = (hi < kCeil) ? min(float(hi) * kInvScale, pc.highRef)
                                       : pc.highRef;
            inc = 2.0 * dLow + (pc.highRef - dHigh);
        } else if (pc.countUnresolved != 0) {
            // Flat, but the outlet front never arrived. This cell keeps no
            // gradient and stays a sink. Counted rather than shrugged off: an
            // unresolved flat looks completely plausible in the render and
            // silently truncates every catchment upstream of it.
            atomicAdd(diag[0], 1u);
        }
    }

    routeField[i] = filledField[i] + inc * pc.flatStep;
}
